    "id": "lem:parametric-label-floor",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "There is a positive numerical \\(c\\), independent of \\(m,d\\) and depending on \\(\\epsilon\\) only through the requirement \\(\\epsilon<1/2\\), such that for every \\(n\\ge1\\), \\(m\\ge0\\), and \\(d\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n R_\\epsilon(n,m,d)\\ge c/n.                                        \\tag{C39}\n\\]\nThe two hypotheses have the same treatment--covariate marginal, so the bound is entirely label-gated.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(R_\\epsilon(n,m,d)\\)",
      "\\(\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(m\\)",
      "\\(d\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:overlap",
      "ass:labeled-iid",
      "ass:auxiliary-iid",
      "ass:sample-independence",
      "def:model-class",
      "def:ate-functional",
      "def:annotation-experiment",
      "def:minimax-risk"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Concentrate \\(X\\) on cell one, take \\(e_1=1/2\\), \\(\\mu_{01}=0\\), and compare \\(\\mu_{11}=1/2-h\\) with \\(\\mu_{11}=1/2+h\\), where \\(h=(16\\sqrt n)^{-1}\\).  Both laws satisfy occupied-cell overlap because \\(\\epsilon<1/2\\), and all unused alphabet cells are null.  Their \\(P_{XA}\\) marginals coincide, whereas their ATEs differ by \\(2h\\).\n\nFor one labeled record, direct summation over the two treated outcome atoms gives\n\\[\n \\chi^2(P_1,P_0)=2h^2\\left\\{(1/2-h)^{-1}+(1/2+h)^{-1}\\right\\}\n \\le16h^2.                                                         \\tag{C40}\n\\]\nProduct factorization and \\(1+z\\le e^z\\) imply\n\\[\n \\chi^2(P_1^{\\otimes n},P_0^{\\otimes n})\n =(1+\\chi^2(P_1,P_0))^n-1\\le e^{16nh^2}-1<1/4.                    \\tag{C41}\n\\]\nThe auxiliary product factors are identical.  Cauchy--Schwarz therefore gives total variation below \\(1/4\\).  Thresholding any estimator at the midpoint of the two ATEs turns it into a test; on a testing error its squared loss under one of the two laws is at least \\(h^2\\).  The sum of testing errors is at least one minus total variation.  Hence the larger of the two risks is at least \\(3h^2/8\\), which proves (C39).",
